111	7	The main strength of this policy is that it protects academic integrity. It makes expectations clear \nand prevents students from replacing their own thinking with AI -generated work. It is also easier \nto enforce in traditional assessments where the purpose i s to measure individual knowledge and \nskill. \nHowever, this approach has weaknesses. It may ignore the reality that AI tools are already widely \navailable. It can discourage students from learning how to use AI responsibly. It may also create \nfear rather than transparency, making students less likely t o admit when they used AI for \nbrainstorming, grammar checking, or feedback. \nA disclosed-use policy  allows students to use AI in clearly defined ways, provided that they \nacknowledge how it was used. For example, UCL recognizes that generative AI is transformative \nand has not banned it completely. Instead, instructors are expected to explain what is allo wed in \neach assignment, and students should acknowledge AI use when it is	16	33	[-0.08590159,0.004720731,0.03903088,-0.04226108,0.028129704,0.07274,0.039933912,-0.02535866,0.0557717,0.110850945,0.04376185,0.028099576,-0.02520065,0.01431565,0.026706098,0.045829706,-0.0067505967,0.004606212,-0.06767151,-0.043622352,0.053665735,0.009111815,-0.0221268,0.02211061,-0.015071975,0.060965493,0.020350682,-0.11344807,0.021737231,-0.009369446,0.0088672275,-0.0008771998,0.024689205,0.018206792,-0.068640426,-0.022107972,-0.08557277,-0.024558445,0.007694544,-0.036034662,-0.05641524,-0.06148142,-0.013130177,0.0028208971,0.05990142,0.0031472559,-0.028711187,-0.07439962,0.0022240523,0.038888093,-0.025631534,-0.002011913,0.048808824,-0.06427172,-0.009126654,-0.02891066,0.04503134,0.004471592,0.01016519,-0.0032020286,-0.039580796,-0.070499964,-0.004402622,-0.054914743,-0.029330336,-0.022413319,-0.10495361,0.013232913,0.0070951795,0.07283775,-0.013822452,0.05954072,0.050238896,0.046130467,0.049813993,0.004893453,-0.0059826863,-0.015028438,0.11257103,-0.07637897